// 023 — Null / None / nil values
// AiLang has no language-level `null` keyword. For Address-typed
// variables, the null pointer is just integer 0 — an Allocate that
// fails returns 0, a not-found lookup can return 0, and checking for
// absence is `EqualTo(addr, 0)`. For "is there a value here" on an
// Integer variable, use Library.Option (see demo 052) — that's the
// explicit, tagged approach.
//
// Notice what's NOT here: there's no `null` that's silently mutable
// into something non-null, no NullPointerException runtime class, no
// billion-dollar mistake. Null is just 0. If you want to carry "no
// value" for a non-Address type, wrap it in Option.
LibraryImport.Arena

SubRoutine.Main {
    // An Address we deliberately leave null.
    p = 0
    IfCondition EqualTo(p, 0) ThenBlock: {
        PrintMessage("p is null\n")
    }

    // Allocate makes it non-null.
    p = Allocate(4)
    IfCondition NotEqual(p, 0) ThenBlock: {
        PrintMessage("p is now a real pointer\n")
    }
}
RunTask(Main)
